# Issue #947, the other direction of `a-matched-delimiter-is-never-reported-unmatched`.
#
# That fixture requires the unmatched-delimiter class never to appear on delimiters that are
# matched. A parser could satisfy it by never emitting the class at all — which would meet the
# letter of the MUST NOT while destroying real error reporting — so this pins the opposite: a
# delimiter that genuinely has no partner IS still reported, for all three kinds and in both
# directions.
#
# **The order of these two groups is load-bearing.** Whether a delimiter is matched is a property of
# the source, so it is decided by a balance walk over the whole token stream, and that walk does not
# stop at statement boundaries: a stray `)` written BELOW an unclosed `(` would pair with it and
# neither would be reported. Stray closers therefore come first, where there is nothing above them
# to pair with, and unclosed openers come last, where there is nothing below. Reordering this file
# would silently weaken it — which is itself the clearest statement of how the rule works.

# Stray closers, with no opener anywhere above them.
print 1 )
print 1 ]
print 1 }

# Unclosed openers, with no closer anywhere below them.
print ( 1 + 2
print [ 1 2
print { a: 1
print :nums[1
